      "math_academy_id": "q-158455",
      "sequence_position": 4,
      "problem": "Compute $P(-1<X<1)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{28}({x}^{2}+1),\\, & -2\\le x\\le 2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a<X<b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1<X<1) & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{3}{28}({x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{28}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{2}+1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{28}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+x]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{28}([\\frac{1}{3}(1)^{3}+(1)]-[\\frac{1}{3}(-1)^{3}+(-1)]) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{28}([\\frac{1}{3}+1]-[-\\frac{1}{3}-1]) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{28}\\cdot \\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
